<p>
Bozhidar Batsov has an excellent post that <a href="https://emacsredux.com/blog/2026/04/07/stealing-from-the-best-emacs-configs/">collects several configuration hacks from a variety of people and distributions</a>. It&rsquo;s a long list and rather than list them all, I&rsquo;m going to mention just a few that appeal to me. Some of them I&rsquo;m already using. Other&rsquo;s I didn&rsquo;t know about but will probably adopt.
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>Save the clipboard before killing: I&rsquo;ve been using this for years. What it does is to make sure that the contents of the system clipboard aren&rsquo;t lost if you do a kill in Emacs. This is much more useful than it sounds, especially if, like me, your do a lot of cutting and pasting from other applications.</li>
<li>Save the kill ring across sessions: I&rsquo;m not sure I&rsquo;ll adopt this but it&rsquo;s easy to see how it could be useful.</li>
<li>Auto-Chmod spripts: Every time I see this one I resolve to add it to my config but always forget. What it does is automatically make scripts (files beginning with <code>#!</code>) executable when they&rsquo;re saved.</li>
<li>Proportional window resizing: When a window is split, this causes all the windows in the frame to resize proportionally.</li>
<li>Faster mark popping. It&rsquo;s sort of like repeat mode for popping the mark ring. After the first <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>u</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> you can continue popping the ring with a simple <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></li>
<li>Auto-select Help window: This is my favorite.When I invoke help, I almost always want to interact with the Help buffer if only to quit and delete it with a <code>q</code>. Unfortunately, the Help buffer doesn&rsquo;t get focus so I have to do a change window to it. This simple configuration gives the Help buffer focus when you open it.</li>
</ul>
<p>
Everybody&rsquo;s needs and preferences are different, of course, so be sure to take a look at Bastov&rsquo;s post to see which ones might be helpful to you.</p>
